=== 1. GSE84727: validating DNAm age in the 665 samples with chronological age ===
  Comparable samples: 665 / 847
  Correlation r = 0.940; mean difference +4.9 years; median absolute error 5.0 years
    control: n = 405, r = 0.931, mean difference +5.0 years
    case: n = 260, r = 0.949, mean difference +4.7 years
  Interpretation: the proxy is considered usable at r of 0.85 or above; this case passes
  Difference in estimation bias between the two groups: -0.39 years, p = 0.272  No difference in bias between the groups; the proxy introduces no group-related bias

=== 2. Age source of the ten cohorts ===
  GSE145361  Parkinson's disease round 1  uses DNAm age  no chronological age field in this cohort
  GSE111629  Parkinson's disease round 1  uses chronological age  chronological age complete
  GSE87648   inflammatory bowel disease round 1  uses chronological age  chronological age complete
  GSE112611  inflammatory bowel disease round 1  uses chronological age  chronological age complete
  GSE84727   schizophrenia round 2  uses DNAm age  chronological age missing in 21.5%, biased towards cases
  GSE80417   schizophrenia round 2  uses chronological age  chronological age missing in 5.8%, unrelated to group
  GSE198904  depression    round 2  uses chronological age  chronological age complete
  GSE273970  depression    round 2  uses DNAm age  age only in ten-year groups
  GSE338167  Alzheimer's disease round 2  uses chronological age  chronological age complete
  GSE144858  Alzheimer's disease round 2  uses chronological age  chronological age complete

  Using DNAm age: 3 sets, using chronological age: 7
  Age source table written, to be cited by the study plan and the Methods

=== 3. Two notes for the Methods ===
  (1) In the two Alzheimer's disease cohorts DNAm age and chronological age correlate at only about 0.65, 
      below the 0.82 to 0.94 of the other cohorts. Both use chronological age, so the analysis is unaffected; 
      the low value may reflect compression of the Horvath clock at old age, plus the imputation of
      1015 and 586 reference sites during normalisation.
  (2) The number of usable sites for the smoking score differs between cohorts (163 to 187), which shifts the scores as a whole; 
      they are not comparable across cohorts. As a within-cohort covariate the shift is absorbed by the intercept and does not matter.

